1794959004764066	sippycup-2u2m2v	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-za4	{"description":"Problem\nThe 2026-11-17 dress rehearsal showed that Sippycup's analysis side is far ahead of its execution side. The offline gates all passed, yet the first attempt to run the documented live sequence against a standards-compliant SIP stack hit five defects that only the bundled loopback fixtures had hidden (fixed in 526b854). What remains is not broken, but it is manual: the baseline call is seven steps across two terminals with sudo and a hand-written PJSUA configuration, the automated call cannot log in, and Quad's scope must be copied by hand into three files.\n\nOutcome\nAn operator can rehearse the exact live sequence locally against a realistic target, then run the approved baseline against staging with one command, from one scope file.\n\nChildren\n- a standards-compliant local practice target;\n- a single-command guided baseline call;\n- a credential-safe softphone campaign runner;\n- one scope intake file that generates every derived artifact;\n- hostname/SNI-pinned TLS targets;\n- an evaluation of a modern replacement for sipsak.\n\nGuardrails\nNothing here weakens the approval model. Every target-facing command stays behind the strict target profile or a frozen, manifest-locked plan; credentials never reach argv, plans, events, or the journal; the practice target is local-only and can never be mistaken for an authorized destination.","kind":"goal","labels":["live","operator","ux","roadmap"],"parent":"bn-22t","size":"l","title":"Make live operation a rehearsed single-command workflow"}	blake3:ieWFLUrifNdBzmeNgzk5ZZUqY3UB3bAm2sH-tZLzJ3g
1794959012977236	sippycup-2u2m2v	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-yfj	{"description":"Problem\nThe only bundled SIP peer is bin/campaign-loopback-uas, a few dozen lines of Python that answer at the datagram source port and ignore Via, authentication, registration, and media. Because it is not a compliant stack, the campaign preflight's missing rport, the unresolvable host-network hostname, and the closed container stdin all passed every July gate. The 2026-11-17 rehearsal had to improvise a target from PJSUA in auto-answer mode, which still cannot challenge for credentials or act as a registrar.\n\nOutcome\nAn optional image and launcher command (for example `./bin/sippycup practice-target up|down|status`) that runs a real SIP server on loopback or a private container network, kept out of the core image like the WebRTC peer. Asterisk is the obvious candidate; record the choice and pin its version.\n\nIt provides:\n- a registrar with Digest authentication and one throwaway test account generated at start, never checked in;\n- UDP, TCP, and TLS signaling with a locally generated certificate carrying both DNS and IP subject alternative names;\n- an echo destination, a destination that plays a known canary, and a destination that rejects;\n- plain RTP and SDES-SRTP media, with PCMU/PCMA at minimum;\n- a matching approved target profile, campaign manifest, and oracle expectations for the practice address, so the complete operator sequence can be run unmodified.\n\nAcceptance\n- The full documented sequence (rehearse, preflight, capture, softphone call with registration, report, triage, assert, evidence lint) passes against it from a clean checkout, in isolated mode, with no sudo.\n- A regression gate runs the campaign preflight, the launcher preflight, and one authenticated call against it; reverting any of the 526b854 fixes makes that gate fail.\n- It binds only loopback or a private container network and refuses to start on a routable address.\n- Planner placeholder rules still reject it as a campaign target outside the explicit practice path, so a practice artifact cannot authorize real traffic.\n- `make full-gate` includes the new gate; `doctor` reports whether the profile is installed.\n\nNotes\nThe softphone registration options documented in docs/CALL-CHECKLIST.md are currently unverified because no registrar exists in the image; this bone closes that.","kind":"task","labels":["testing","fixtures","sip","live"],"parent":"bn-za4","size":"l","title":"Add a standards-compliant local practice target with registrar, auth, and TLS"}	blake3:qcGpo5xivbm2tnJReiN_SMg_F-ZobDF3tXVmjvU7HBQ
1794959013084968	sippycup-2u2m2v	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2ky	{"description":"Problem\n`sippycup one-call` only prints the reviewed sequence. The operator then runs seven steps by hand across two terminals: sudo host capture, preflight, a PJSUA call driven by a hand-written configuration file, Ctrl-C, report, triage, and assert. Step 4 was undocumented until 526b854. The only executor that exists is the MCP `execute_one_call` tool (bn-2m2), which is agent-only, disabled by default, credential-free, and places SIPp's audio-less built-in call.\n\nOutcome\nA human-facing command, for example `./bin/sippycup call PROFILE --dial DESTINATION`, that executes the already-reviewed one-call plan in one terminal:\n1. rehearse the profile and refuse unless it is ready now (approved, unexpired, literal address);\n2. start a target-scoped capture inside the isolated container, where rootless capture works without sudo;\n3. send the single OPTIONS preflight and stop on failure;\n4. register if an account is supplied, place exactly one softphone call, play a canary or operator-supplied WAV, and record the returned audio;\n5. hang up at the profile's duration limit, stop capture, and confirm no media continues;\n6. write a mode-0700 run directory with capture, softphone log, recorded audio, report, triage, assertion result, and evidence manifest;\n7. print a short verdict and the exact journal command to record it.\n\nDesign notes\n- Build the softphone step as a reusable driver (PJSUA configured through a private file or stdin, never argv); the campaign softphone runner reuses it.\n- Take the password only from --secret-env, --secret-fd, or a provider, matching campaign execute.\n- Generate oracle expectations from the profile's approved addresses rather than asking the operator to write them.\n- `--dry-run` prints the same plan `one-call` prints today and sends nothing.\n- Ctrl-C follows the campaign emergency-stop contract: stop the call, flush the capture, keep the evidence.\n- Isolated mode sends through the runtime's NAT; say so in the output and report one-way media as a NAT finding, not a target defect.\n\nAcceptance\n- Against the local practice target, one command produces a passing run with bidirectional media, an authenticated registration, and a clean evidence lint.\n- A pending, expired, revoked, or hostname-only profile sends zero packets.\n- No credential appears in argv, the run directory, events, or logs (reuse the fixture-secret test pattern).\n- Exactly one OPTIONS and one call are sent; the capture filter contains only approved addresses.\n- The command is registered in config/commands.tsv with activity `network` and has network-free help.\n\nThis is the implementation that the guided live exit gate bn-guh then proves against staging once Quad approves.","kind":"task","labels":["live","operator","softphone","safety"],"parent":"bn-za4","size":"l","title":"Add a single-command guided baseline call that executes the one-call plan"}	blake3:DHWmgBN11W1tM7vO8Wd88zJ3JMjzV6g5WZ-aj2Bjm3I
1794959013194537	sippycup-2u2m2v	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1c5	{"description":"Problem\nbin/campaign-sipp-runner refuses any step that carries credentials, because SIPp's password option would expose the value in argv. Its `call` step is SIPp's built-in `uac` scenario: one unauthenticated INVITE to the user `service` with no audio. Against a platform that requires accounts or a specific destination, every generated campaign case is rejected before it tests anything, and the matrix factors (codec, DTMF, hold, teardown initiator, duration) are recorded but never exercised.\n\nOutcome\nA second bundled runner built on the softphone driver from the guided baseline call. It:\n- receives the step and credentials in the existing one-shot stdin envelope and passes them to PJSUA through a private file or descriptor, never argv;\n- dials a destination user taken from the manifest (add an explicit, schema-validated field; do not default to `service`);\n- registers when the target requires it, then plays and records canary audio so the packet oracle and media analyzer have real media to judge;\n- honors the per-step duration budget and exits non-zero on any unexpected final status;\n- maps the factors it can actually perform (codec, RFC 4733 DTMF, caller teardown, duration) and reports the rest as not exercised, so reports never claim unobserved behavior.\n\nRelated prior work: bn-3jh generates learned SIPp packs with named secret references; decide whether the new runner replaces or complements that path and record why.\n\nAcceptance\n- An authenticated one-call campaign succeeds against the local practice target under `--isolated campaign execute`, with bidirectional RTP in the stripped capture.\n- The fixture-secret test passes for the new runner: the value is absent from argv, environment of other tools, events, and every artifact.\n- A wrong password produces exactly one failed attempt and stops the campaign (no retry, no lockout risk).\n- The existing SIPp runner remains the default for credential-free plans; runner selection is fixed by the plan, not by an arbitrary path on the command line.\n- docs/CAMPAIGN-MANIFEST.md known limitations are updated.","kind":"task","labels":["campaigns","credentials","softphone","live"],"parent":"bn-za4","size":"m","title":"Add a credential-safe softphone campaign runner that dials a real destination"}	blake3:gsKbTuF5NqzRq9Knl9CajB8eig2O1vhBNbZuT8IQnKY
1794959013303770	sippycup-2u2m2v	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-39v	{"description":"Problem\nQuad's written scope currently has to be transcribed by hand into three independent files that can drift apart: the target profile (approval, addresses, one-call limits), the campaign manifest (networks, ports, transports, ceilings, stop conditions, targets), and the oracle expectations (allowed endpoints, codecs). config/target.env.example is a fourth, purely advisory worksheet. docs/CALL-CHECKLIST.md lists what to ask for, but nothing turns the answers into artifacts or shows what is still missing. Transcription is the most likely source of operator error on a live day.\n\nOutcome\nOne private scope file under the engagement directory (for example `work/ENGAGEMENT/scope.yaml`) as the single source of truth, plus:\n- `./bin/sippycup scope questions` renders a plain question sheet to send to the service owner: approval identifier and validity window, literal signaling and media addresses, ports and transports, whether calls need an account and how the password is delivered, destination to dial, RTP or SRTP, codecs and DTMF method, call/rate/packet ceilings, stop conditions, NAT handling for clients behind NAT, live contact, retention and disclosure;\n- `./bin/sippycup scope check` reports exactly which answers are missing or inconsistent, without network activity;\n- `./bin/sippycup scope compile` generates the target profile, a baseline campaign manifest, and oracle expectations, each stamped with the scope file's SHA-256 so the advisor can detect a stale derivative.\n\nAcceptance\n- Derived files pass `rehearse`, `campaign plan`, and the expectations loader unchanged.\n- Editing the scope file without recompiling makes `status` report the derivatives as stale.\n- The scope file holds credential reference names only; a password-shaped value is rejected.\n- Nothing is invented: an unanswered question leaves the profile pending and blocks compilation of the affected artifact.\n- Unquoted YAML timestamps are accepted, as in the profile loader.\n- The engagement advisor recommends `scope check` first when no scope file exists, and config/target.env.example is retired or generated.","kind":"task","labels":["authorization","operator","ux"],"parent":"bn-za4","size":"m","title":"Compile profile, manifest, and expectations from one scope intake file"}	blake3:k318YS85U5Poxq9Ne6eXjeKvU2sWNCzTZNeQadrVxPM
1794959013415977	sippycup-2u2m2v	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1bi	{"description":"Problem\nTwo deliberate safety choices combine into a practical dead end for SIP over TLS:\n- the one-call planner blocks unless target.host is itself a literal approved address, so a profile cannot name the hostname a certificate was issued for;\n- the campaign TLS preflight validates the certificate against the frozen literal address, so it fails unless the certificate carries that IP as a subject alternative name, which staging certificates rarely do.\ndocs/WORKBENCH.md already notes that hostname/SNI pinning needs a separate reviewed transport feature. Until it exists, a TLS-only staging service cannot pass rehearsal or preflight without weakening validation by hand.\n\nOutcome\nLet a profile and a campaign target carry an explicit, reviewed TLS server name alongside the pinned literal address. Connections still go only to the approved literal address; the name is used solely for SNI, certificate verification, and the SIP request URI/domain. No DNS lookup is introduced.\n\nAcceptance\n- A target with address 192.0.2.10 and server name sip.staging.example connects to the literal address, sends that SNI, and verifies the certificate against that name.\n- A certificate for a different name fails closed; there is no ignore-failure switch in the profile, plan, or campaign path. (The launcher preflight's existing sipsak --tls-ignore-cert-failure is reviewed and either removed or reported as an explicit unverified result.)\n- The server name is frozen into the plan and covered by the manifest lock; changing it requires recompiling.\n- Rehearsal and the one-call plan become ready for a hostname-fronted TLS target that has a literal approved address.\n- Verified against the practice target's certificate in both matching and mismatching cases.","kind":"task","labels":["tls","authorization","live"],"parent":"bn-za4","size":"m","title":"Support hostname and SNI pinned TLS targets on literal approved addresses"}	blake3:q_pydoCqg2AjClT6wfvYaCCwiASGbW-Munf1Cl_ylN8
1794959013523799	sippycup-2u2m2v	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-fl0	{"description":"Problem\nThe launcher preflight and the campaign OPTIONS step both depend on sipsak 0.9.8.1, which is effectively unmaintained. It insists on resolving the container's own hostname before sending (the cause of the host-network preflight failure fixed in 526b854), prints a spurious destination-port warning on every run, and its exit codes are coarse.\n\nOutcome\nA short written decision, not a migration by default. Evaluate one or two maintained SIP command-line clients (sipexer is the first candidate; it has not yet been checked against this image) for: Digest authentication without secrets in argv, UDP/TCP/TLS and WebSocket transports, deterministic exit codes, machine-readable output, static build size, and license. Compare against simply replacing sipsak with the in-tree Python OPTIONS transaction already used by the campaign preflight.\n\nAcceptance\n- A recorded recommendation: keep sipsak, replace it with a named pinned tool, or drop it for the in-tree transaction.\n- If replacing, a follow-up bone with the pinned version and checksum; no tool is added to the image by this bone.\n\nLow priority: sipsak works today.","kind":"task","labels":["tooling","decision"],"parent":"bn-za4","size":"s","title":"Decide whether to replace sipsak with a maintained SIP client","urgency":"punt"}	blake3:F8-5Na8UHXhri1j3yu8JswDytACm-eKY24jWz6QwTZo
1794959013630417	sippycup-2u2m2v	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-ww5	{"description":"Problem\nThe goal promised `sippycup torture plan|run|minimize`. The state-aware runner (bn-1kk) and minimizer exist as a library and are proven against isolated AF_UNIX reference endpoints, but the CLI exposes only corpus, exit-gate, review-template, validate-review, and plan. There is no network transport adapter and no `run` command, so the corpus cannot be sent to an authorized staging target at all. README and docs describe it as \"for later use by the guarded state-aware runner\".\n\nOutcome\nA target-facing `torture run` (and `minimize` on a reproduced failure) that plugs a real UDP/TCP/TLS transport into the existing runner callbacks, inside the same capture-to-report evidence lifecycle campaigns use.\n\nRequirements\n- Refuse to start without all three: a ready target profile, an approved owner defaults review that validates against the current corpus and technical-gate digests (bn-151), and a frozen torture plan locked to both.\n- Establish dialog state with the softphone or SIPp driver, inject exactly one case, classify the response, then place the clean recovery canary before any further case.\n- Enforce the reviewed defaults as hard ceilings (1 case, 6 packets, 8192 bytes, 1 Hz, concurrency 1, 30 s, 1 failure, 5 s action timeout); flags can only lower them.\n- Capture is mandatory and target-scoped; the watchdog stops on any ceiling, capture death, operator signal, or failed recovery canary.\n- No credential guessing, spoofed sources, reflection, or amplification paths.\n\nAcceptance\n- Against the local practice target and a deliberately fragile reference endpoint, the run sends bit-exact corpus bytes, stops at the first recovery failure, and leaves a reproducible evidence directory.\n- A pending or mismatched defaults review sends zero packets.\n- Sequencing: a successful baseline call against the same target must exist in the engagement before a torture run is admitted.","kind":"task","labels":["live","robustness","safety","authorization"],"parent":"bn-3ka","size":"l","title":"Wire the torture runner to a live transport and expose torture run"}	blake3:iRT3E-n6pE5IOz84T5k45EKlNQDaeu6Fusray4iHZa0
1794959013735172	sippycup-2u2m2v	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2u5	{"description":"Problem\nThe capacity goal bn-1yq closed with `envelope plan` and `envelope run`, but `run` is a deterministic, network-free controller simulator. It reviews timing, budgets, and pause/stop precedence; it places no calls and consumes no live observations. No command can currently measure a real degradation knee or recovery against staging.\n\nOutcome\nA live mode for `envelope run` that drives real calls through the campaign execution lifecycle, feeds SIP outcomes, setup latency, and RTP loss/jitter from the live capture into the existing change-point and recovery logic, and backs off as the simulator already models.\n\nRequirements\n- Separate written approval for load: every intensity, total-call, duration, hold, cooldown, and recovery maximum comes from the frozen envelope plan; flags can only lower them.\n- Uses the credential-safe softphone runner when the target requires accounts.\n- Stops new calls immediately on a hard threshold, operator signal, capture failure, or health-adapter failure, then measures recovery with one-call canaries.\n- Reports a censored result when the authorized ceiling is reached first and never labels an untested value as capacity.\n\nAcceptance\n- Against a local practice target with an injected degradation, the live run backs off before the hard ceiling and reports a knee interval comparable to the simulator's for the same plan.\n- A healthy target stops cleanly at the authorized ceiling.\n\nDeliberately deferred: this is the highest-volume traffic Sippycup can generate. Do not start until a baseline call and a small authenticated campaign have succeeded against staging and the owner has approved explicit load limits.","kind":"task","labels":["live","load","safety","authorization"],"size":"l","title":"Drive real calls from envelope run instead of the controller simulator","urgency":"punt"}	blake3:uLgeLF9EZ83LYn70BKjIyE_XAkC9KTe9wsr8Rhai76c
